Yes — I actually prefer your approach for this project.

Instead of finding one giant NLP course and watching 4–10 hours of it, do one concept at a time, understand it, practice it, and then move on.

For your spam classifier, I'd structure your NLP learning like this:

Day 1 — Text preprocessing

Learn:

What is text preprocessing?

Lowercasing

Removing punctuation

Handling whitespace

Normalization

What should/shouldn't be removed


Don't blindly apply every preprocessing technique. For spam detection, things like punctuation, numbers, and capitalization can sometimes carry useful information.


---

Day 2 — Tokenization ⭐

Learn:

What is a token?

Word tokenization

Sentence tokenization

Character tokenization

How tokenization works


Example:

"Congratulations! You won $5000."

             ↓

["Congratulations", "You", "won", "$", "5000"]

The important thing is to understand why we tokenize, not just how to call a library function.


---

Day 3 — Stop Words

Learn:

What are stop words?

Why remove them?

Advantages/disadvantages

When you should not remove them


This last part is important. Stop-word removal isn't universally beneficial; some common words can still carry information for certain classification tasks. 


---

Day 4 — Stemming

Understand:

playing
played
plays

→ approximately:

play

Learn:

How stemming works

Porter Stemmer

Advantages

Problems with stemming


Don't spend too much time here.


---

Day 5 — Lemmatization

Learn:

What is lemmatization?

Stemming vs lemmatization

WordNet lemmatizer

Why lemmatization is more linguistically meaningful


Again, understand the concept rather than memorizing APIs.


---

Day 6 — Bag of Words ⭐⭐⭐

This is where things get really relevant to your ML model.

Learn how:

"I love dogs"
"I love cats"

becomes something like:

I   love   dogs   cats

Text1   1    1      1      0
Text2   1    1      0      1

You're learning how to turn text into numerical features that your Logistic Regression/Naive Bayes model can actually consume. This is essentially what scikit-learn's text feature extraction tools are designed to do. 


---

Day 7 — N-grams

Learn:

Unigram:
"win", "money"

Bigram:
"win money"

Trigram:
"you won money"

This is especially useful for spam detection because phrases can be more informative than individual words.

For example:

"claim"

vs

"claim your reward"


---

Day 8 — TF-IDF ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Spend the most time here.

Understand:

Term Frequency

Document Frequency

Inverse Document Frequency

Why common words get lower importance

Why distinctive words get higher importance

How TF-IDF produces numerical vectors


The core idea is that simply counting words isn't always enough; TF-IDF reweights words so that terms appearing in many documents have less influence and more distinctive terms have more. 

Then learn:

TfidfVectorizer()

and understand:

fit()
transform()
fit_transform()


---

Then you're DONE with basic NLP 🎯

Your complete pipeline becomes:

TEXT
                  ↓
          Preprocessing
                  ↓
             Tokenization
                  ↓
              TF-IDF
                  ↓
        Numerical feature vector
                  ↓
       ┌──────────┴──────────┐
       ↓                     ↓
Logistic Regression     Naive Bayes
       ↓                     ↓
       └──────────┬──────────┘
                  ↓
          SPAM / NOT SPAM

And one cool thing: you don't necessarily need to manually implement every preprocessing step yourself. Scikit-learn's CountVectorizer and TfidfVectorizer already handle tokenization and several preprocessing/vectorization operations for you. 

So your learning strategy should be:

> Understand the NLP concept first → implement a tiny example → then see how scikit-learn does it.



That's much better than watching a 4-hour video and copying TfidfVectorizer() without knowing what's happening underneath. 😄

After TF-IDF, I'd stop the theory and build your first spam classifier. Then we'll learn anything else only when the project actually needs it.
